# Contributing a client-side adapter

Fusor is the **inbound delivery plane** (see [`ARCHITECTURE.md`](../ARCHITECTURE.md)):
it accepts a webhook from any platform, never parses the body, and delivers the
raw request to a customer's SDK — over a WebSocket stream or an HTTP POST. All
platform-specific knowledge (which platform this is, how to check its signature,
what its payload means) lives on the other side of that boundary, in a
**client-side adapter**.

A client-side adapter is the SDK-side code that:

1. **consumes** Fusor's delivery (`RawInboundEvent`),
2. **verifies** the platform's signature and **parses** the raw request into a
   typed payload,
3. **maps** that payload to Spectrum messages your app receives, and
4. **dispatches** outbound replies/sends back through the platform's API.

This guide explains the contract Fusor gives an adapter and walks through
building one. The reference adapter is
[`@photon-ai/linq`](https://github.com/photon-hq/linq); the contract and
authoring API live in the Spectrum SDK,
[`spectrum-ts`](https://github.com/photon-hq/spectrum-ts).

> **Adapters do not live in this repo.** Fusor is intentionally
> platform-agnostic — adding a platform needs *zero* changes to ingress,
> fanout, the proto, or infra. You contribute an adapter to the SDK or as a
> standalone package; this doc lives here because Fusor *defines the wire
> contract* every adapter consumes.

---

## Where the pieces live

| Repo | Role | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **fusor** (this repo) | The delivery plane. Receives webhooks, fans out `RawInboundEvent`. No adapters. | `apps/ingress`, `apps/fanout-websocket`, `apps/fanout-webhook` |
| **spectrum-ts** (SDK) | Defines the adapter contract (`defineFusorPlatform`, `fusor()`), hosts first-party providers, consumes Fusor's delivery. | `src/platform`, `src/fusor`, `src/providers/*` |
| **adapter packages** | A standalone provider for one platform. | `@photon-ai/linq` |

> **The contract is language-agnostic.** Anything that can speak the WebSocket
> stream or accept the webhook POST can be an adapter. This guide follows the
> reference path — the TypeScript SDK and its `defineFusorPlatform` API. To
> consume the raw stream directly in another language, the WebSocket contract
> is fully specified in
> [`apps/fanout-websocket/protocol.md`](../apps/fanout-websocket/protocol.md).

---

## What Fusor delivers to your adapter

Fusor offers two transports for the *same* event; a well-built adapter supports
both (the SDK handles the transport — you write one pipeline). See
[`spectrum-ts`'s `docs/fusor.md`](https://github.com/photon-hq/spectrum-ts) for
the consumer-side detail.

| Transport | Your adapter receives | Fusor service |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Streaming** | a stream of `RawInboundEvent` over a long-lived WebSocket subscription (SDK dials Fusor) | [`fanout-websocket`](../apps/fanout-websocket/protocol.md) |
| **Webhook** | a protobuf `RawInboundEvent` POSTed to your HTTPS endpoint (Fusor calls you) | [`fanout-webhook`](../apps/fanout-webhook/BEHAVIOR.md) |

### `RawInboundEvent`

The envelope (`@photon-ai/proto/photon/fusor/v1/inbound`). The fields an adapter
cares about:

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `eventId` | UUIDv7 set by ingress; mirrored to JetStream `Nats-Msg-Id`. Use for end-to-end dedup. |
| `projectId` | The project that owns this event. |
| `platform` | The platform string (see [routing](#platform-routing--the-name-invariant)). How the SDK picks your adapter. |
| `receivedAt` | Wall-clock time ingress accepted the request. |
| `sourceId` | Optional channel-side source id (e.g. a Slack `api_app_id`). |
| `rawRequest` | **The original HTTP/1.1 request, bit-exact** — request line + headers + body. This is what you verify and parse. |

### `rawRequest` — the bytes you verify

`rawRequest` is the inbound webhook serialized to HTTP/1.1 wire format:

```
<METHOD> <path>[?<query>] HTTP/1.1\r\n
Header-Name: header-value\r\n
...\r\n
\r\n
<body bytes, bit-exact>
```

Ingress has already stripped a denylist of framing / load-balancer / CDN headers
(`host`, `content-length`, `x-forwarded-*`, `cf-*`, `x-amzn-*`, …), so what
remains is suitable for HMAC verification — the platform's own signature headers
(`x-slack-signature`, `x-hub-signature-256`, etc.) ride through untouched. The
exact denylist and construction are in
[`apps/ingress/BEHAVIOR.md`](../apps/ingress/BEHAVIOR.md). The SDK parses these
bytes for you and hands `verify` a `{ headers, method, path, rawBody }` request.

### Platform routing & the name invariant

The platform is the **first path segment of the webhook URL**, lowercased and
validated against `^[a-z0-9_-]+$` by ingress. It is copied verbatim onto
`event.platform` and used as the third token of the NATS subject
`inbound.<projectId>.<platform>`. There is **no closed list** — Fusor doesn't
know or care which platforms exist.

The SDK routes each delivery to your adapter by `event.platform`, so **the same
string must appear in three places**:

1. the `defineFusorPlatform(name, …)` name (→ `message.platform`),
2. the `fusor(name, verify)` routing key the SDK registers your handler under,
3. the webhook URL path segment Fusor stamps as `event.platform`.

Pin it to one constant and reuse it ([LinQ's `config.ts`](https://github.com/photon-hq/linq)):

```typescript
/** defineFusorPlatform name + fusor() key + Fusor's inbound event.platform. */
export const ACME_PLATFORM = "acme";
```

### The reply path

For platforms that need a synchronous HTTP reply to the inbound request (Slack's
`url_verification`, WhatsApp's `hub.challenge`), your adapter can produce an
`InboundReply` (`{ status, headers, body, errorReason }`). On the webhook
transport it's the HTTP response; on streaming it flows back over the same
stream that delivered the event. Ingress awaits it for a short window (default
~2.5s) and otherwise falls back to `200`. You set this with `respond(...)` from `messages` — see
[Replies & protocol handshakes](#replies--protocol-handshakes).

---

## The adapter contract at a glance

An adapter is one value built with `defineFusorPlatform(name, def)`:

| Field | Required | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `config` | ✅ | A `zod` object schema for user-supplied config (API keys, secrets, options). |
| `lifecycle.createClient` | ✅ | Returns `fusor(name, verify)` (the inbound brand). Build your outbound client here and cache it on `store`. |
| `lifecycle.destroyClient` | — | Tear down timers/connections on `app.stop()`. |
| `messages` | ✅ | `({ payload, respond }) => ProviderMessageRecord \| record[] \| undefined`. Maps a **verified** payload to message(s); `respond(...)` sets a synchronous reply. |
| `send` | ✅ | `({ space, content, client, config, store }) => ProviderMessageRecord \| undefined`. Dispatches outbound `Content` by `content.type`. |
| `user.resolve` | ✅ | `({ input: { userID } }) => { id }`. |
| `space.resolve` | ✅ | `({ input: { users, params } }) => { id }`. |
| `space.params` | — | A `zod` schema for `space(...)` parameters (e.g. a target chat id). |
| `user/space/message.schema` | — | `zod` schemas describing extra fields on the resolved user / space / message. |
| `actions.getMessage` | — | Fetch a message by id (powers `space.getMessage(id)`). |

`messages` (inbound) and `send` (outbound) are the **two universal contracts** —
99% of an adapter is those two plus `verify`. The relevant SDK source:
`src/platform/define.ts`, `src/fusor/types.ts`, `src/fusor/core.ts`.

---

## Walkthrough

We'll build a small `acme` adapter (TypeScript SDK). A good source layout, from
LinQ:

```
src/
  index.ts        # defineFusorPlatform(...) — the export
  config.ts       # zod config schema + the platform-name constant
  types.ts        # your platform's webhook payload types
  verify.ts       # FusorVerify: HMAC check + parse → payload
  client.ts       # outbound SDK wrapper, cached on `store`
  space.ts        # user/space resolvers
  inbound/        # payload → ProviderMessageRecord
  outbound/       # Content → platform API calls
```

A standalone package declares `spectrum-ts` (and `zod`) as **peers**:

```jsonc
{ "peerDependencies": { "spectrum-ts": ">=1.15.0", "zod": "^4" } }
```

### 1. Config schema

```typescript
// config.ts
import z from "zod";

export const ACME_PLATFORM = "acme";
export const DEFAULT_REPLAY_TOLERANCE_SEC = 300;

export const configSchema = z.object({
  apiKey: z.string().min(1), // outbound API calls
  webhookSigningSecret: z.string().min(1).optional(), // inbound HMAC (see §Signatures)
  replayToleranceSec: z.number().int().positive().default(DEFAULT_REPLAY_TOLERANCE_SEC),
});

export type AcmeConfig = z.infer<typeof configSchema>;
```

### 2. Verify + parse → typed payload

`verify` receives the inbound request unwrapped from `rawRequest`:
`{ headers (lowercased), method, path, rawBody: Uint8Array }`. Authenticate
against the **raw bytes** with a replay guard, then parse. **Throw to reject** —
a throw makes the event *poison* (Fusor returns 400 and does not retry).

```typescript
// verify.ts
import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from "node:crypto";
import type { FusorVerify, FusorVerifyRequest } from "spectrum-ts";
import type { AcmeConfig } from "./config";
import type { AcmePayload } from "./types";

const TS_HEADER = "x-acme-timestamp";
const SIG_HEADER = "x-acme-signature";

const safeEqualHex = (a: string, b: string): boolean => {
  const left = Buffer.from(a, "hex");
  const right = Buffer.from(b, "hex");
  return left.length > 0 && left.length === right.length && timingSafeEqual(left, right);
};

export const makeVerify =
  (config: AcmeConfig): FusorVerify<AcmePayload> =>
  (req: FusorVerifyRequest): AcmePayload => {
    const bodyText = new TextDecoder().decode(req.rawBody);

    if (config.webhookSigningSecret) {
      const ts = req.headers[TS_HEADER];
      const sig = req.headers[SIG_HEADER];
      if (!(ts && sig)) throw new Error("acme webhook missing signature headers");

      const ageSec = Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - Number(ts));
      if (!Number.isFinite(Number(ts)) || ageSec > config.replayToleranceSec) {
        throw new Error("acme webhook timestamp outside tolerance (possible replay)");
      }

      const expected = createHmac("sha256", config.webhookSigningSecret)
        .update(`${ts}.${bodyText}`)
        .digest("hex");
      if (!safeEqualHex(expected, sig)) throw new Error("acme webhook signature mismatch");
    }

    const json = JSON.parse(bodyText) as AcmePayload; // throws → poison, fine
    if (!json.event_type) throw new Error("acme webhook missing event_type");
    return json;
  };
```

> Use `crypto.timingSafeEqual` for the comparison and always replay-guard the
> signed timestamp. Read headers by **lowercase** key — the SDK lowercases them.

### 3. Inbound: payload → message record(s)

`messages` runs **once per verified event**. Return the record(s) to deliver, or
`undefined` for events you don't surface. Build `content` with the authoring
factories from `spectrum-ts/authoring`:

```typescript
// inbound/messages.ts
import { asReaction, asText, type ProviderMessageRecord } from "spectrum-ts/authoring";
import type { AcmePayload } from "../types";

export const handleMessages = ({
  payload,
}: {
  payload: AcmePayload;
}): ProviderMessageRecord | undefined => {
  switch (payload.event_type) {
    case "message.received":
      return {
        id: payload.data.id,
        content: asText(payload.data.text),
        sender: { id: payload.data.from },
        space: { id: payload.data.chat_id },
        timestamp: new Date(payload.data.sent_at),
      };
    case "reaction.added":
      return {
        id: payload.event_id,
        content: asReaction({ emoji: payload.data.emoji, target: stub(payload.data.message_id) }),
        space: { id: payload.data.chat_id },
      };
    default:
      return; // status/echo/etc. — nothing to deliver
  }
};
```

A `ProviderMessageRecord` is `{ id, content, sender?: { id }, space: { id }, timestamp?, ...extras }`.
Spectrum inflates it into a full `Message` with `.reply()` / `.react()`. Notes:

- **`sender` is optional.** Senderless inbound (typing, some reactions) is fine —
  omit `sender`.
- **Reaction targets** take a message reference. When you only have the target's
  id, build a minimal stub — `{ id, content } as unknown as Message` (LinQ's
  `stubMessage`) — and pass it as `target`; Spectrum inflates it on delivery.
- **Return an array** when one event fans out into several messages (text +
  attachments). For a bundle delivered as a unit, use `asGroup({ items })`;
  `Spectrum({ options: { flattenGroups: true } })` splits it back per item.
- **`undefined` means "ignore"** — don't surface your own outbound echoes.

### 4. Outbound: dispatch `Content` by type

`send` inspects `content.type` and calls the platform API. Return a record for
content that produces a message; return `undefined` for fire-and-forget signals.
Throw `UnsupportedError.content(type)` for content you can't express.

```typescript
// outbound/send.ts
import { type Content, UnsupportedError } from "spectrum-ts";
import type { ProviderMessageRecord } from "spectrum-ts/authoring";
import { getClient } from "../client";
import type { AcmeConfig } from "../config";

export const send = async ({
  space,
  content,
  config,
  store,
}: {
  space: { id: string };
  content: Content;
  config: AcmeConfig;
  store: { get(k: string): unknown; set(k: string, v: unknown): void };
}): Promise<ProviderMessageRecord | undefined> => {
  const client = getClient(store, config); // outbound SDK, cached on store (§The store)

  switch (content.type) {
    case "text": {
      const { messageId } = await client.sendMessage(space.id, content.text);
      return { id: messageId, content, space: { id: space.id }, timestamp: new Date() };
    }
    case "reaction":
      await client.addReaction(content.target.id, content.emoji);
      return; // control signal, no message
    case "typing":
      return; // no typing API — no-op so space.responding() stays portable
    default:
      throw UnsupportedError.content(content.type);
  }
};
```

`Content` is a discriminated union (`text`, `attachment`, `voice`, `reaction`,
`reply`, `typing`, `edit`, `rename`, `group`, `poll`, `contact`, `richlink`,
`avatar`, `custom`). Sugar like `message.reply(...)` / `space.startTyping()` all
routes through `send`, so handling the `content.type`s you support is all you
need.

### 5. Resolve users & spaces

```typescript
// space.ts
export const resolveUser = ({ input }: { input: { userID: string } }) =>
  Promise.resolve({ id: input.userID });

export const resolveSpace = ({ input }: { input: { users: { id: string }[] } }) => {
  const id = input.users[0]?.id;
  if (!id) throw new Error("acme space requires at least one recipient");
  return Promise.resolve({ id });
};
```

Resolution can be lazy — LinQ encodes a "pending" marker as `space.id` and
creates the real chat on the first send.

### 6. Wire it up

`createClient` returns `fusor(name, verify)` — the **inbound** brand. There's no
inbound connection to make here (Fusor owns that); build your **outbound** client
and cache it on `store`.

```typescript
// index.ts
import { defineFusorPlatform, fusor } from "spectrum-ts";
import { ACME_PLATFORM, configSchema } from "./config";
import { initClient } from "./client";
import { handleMessages } from "./inbound/messages";
import { send } from "./outbound/send";
import { resolveSpace, resolveUser } from "./space";
import type { AcmePayload } from "./types";
import { makeVerify } from "./verify";

export type { AcmeConfig } from "./config";

export const acme = defineFusorPlatform(ACME_PLATFORM, {
  config: configSchema,
  lifecycle: {
    createClient: ({ config, store }) => {
      initClient(store, config); // build + cache the outbound client
      return Promise.resolve(fusor<AcmePayload>(ACME_PLATFORM, makeVerify(config)));
    },
  },
  user: { resolve: resolveUser },
  space: { resolve: resolveSpace },
  messages: handleMessages,
  send,
});
```

A user then drops it into their app:

```typescript
import { Spectrum, text } from "spectrum-ts";
import { acme } from "@photon-ai/acme";

const app = await Spectrum({
  projectId: process.env.PROJECT_ID,
  projectSecret: process.env.PROJECT_SECRET,
  providers: [acme.config({ apiKey: process.env.ACME_API_KEY })],
});

for await (const [space, message] of app.messages) {
  if (message.content.type === "text") await space.send(text(`echo: ${message.content.text}`));
}
```

---

## The `store` — caching the outbound client

`createClient` returns the inbound `FusorClient`, so the `client` argument later
passed to `send` is that brand — not your platform SDK. Build your outbound
client once in `createClient` and cache it on `store`, a small per-platform
in-memory KV available on every ctx (LinQ's `client.ts`):

```typescript
// client.ts
const CLIENT_KEY = "acme.client";

export const initClient = (store, config) => {
  const client = makeAcmeClient(config); // wraps your platform SDK
  store.set(CLIENT_KEY, client);
  return client;
};

export const getClient = (store, config) =>
  (store.get(CLIENT_KEY) as AcmeClient | undefined) ?? initClient(store, config);
```

---

## Signatures & security

Two independent layers — you implement only the inner one.

| Layer | Secret | Headers | Signed value | Who |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Inner — the event is genuinely from your platform** | `webhookSigningSecret` (your config) | your platform's, e.g. `x-acme-signature` | platform-defined (often `{ts}.{body}`) | **you**, in `verify` |
| **Outer — the POST is genuinely from Fusor** | `webhookSecret` (passed to `Spectrum(...)`) | `x-spectrum-signature` / `x-spectrum-timestamp`, scheme `v0=hex(HMAC-SHA256(secret, "v0:{ts}:" ++ rawBody))` | the raw POST bytes | the **SDK** |

The outer layer is Fusor's outbound signing for the webhook transport (see
[`apps/fanout-webhook/BEHAVIOR.md`](../apps/fanout-webhook/BEHAVIOR.md)); the SDK
verifies it before your code runs (returns **401** on a bad/missing signature
when `webhookSecret` is set). You don't write it. Your adapter owns only the
**inner** platform signature, in `verify`.

---

## Replies & protocol handshakes

Set a synchronous reply with `respond` from `messages`:

```typescript
messages: ({ payload, respond }) => {
  if (payload.event_type === "url_verification") {
    respond({ status: 200, body: payload.challenge });
    return; // nothing to deliver to the app
  }
  // …normal mapping…
},
```

`respond(reply)` takes `{ status?, headers?, body?: string | Uint8Array }` and
flows back through Fusor on both transports. Most adapters call it only for
handshakes.

**Failure semantics (webhook transport):**

| Situation | Returns | Fusor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Success (incl. a `respond(...)` reply) | reply status, or `200` | done |
| Undecodable body / unknown platform / your `verify` threw | `400` (poison) | won't retry |
| Bad/missing **Fusor-origin** signature (`webhookSecret` set) | `401` | won't retry |
| The **consumer's** handler threw | `500` | retries (at-least-once) |

The distinction that matters: a throw in **your** `verify`/`messages` is *poison*
(400, no retry — malformed or unauthentic). A throw in the **app developer's**
handler is transient (500, retried). Don't throw from `messages` for transient
downstream issues — that would wrongly poison a valid event. Because delivery is
at-least-once, keep `send`/`messages` idempotent; dedupe on `message.id`.

---

## Testing

`verify` and your inbound mapper are pure functions — unit-test them with
`vitest` (mirror `test/verify.test.ts` in `@photon-ai/linq`):

```typescript
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";
import { createHmac } from "node:crypto";
import { configSchema } from "../src/config";
import { makeVerify } from "../src/verify";

const SECRET = "whsec_test";
const req = (body: string, headers: Record<string, string>) => ({
  headers, method: "POST", path: "/acme", rawBody: new TextEncoder().encode(body),
});

describe("makeVerify", () => {
  it("accepts a correctly signed, fresh webhook", () => {
    const verify = makeVerify(configSchema.parse({ apiKey: "k", webhookSigningSecret: SECRET }));
    const ts = String(Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000));
    const body = JSON.stringify({ event_type: "message.received", data: {} });
    const sig = createHmac("sha256", SECRET).update(`${ts}.${body}`).digest("hex");
    expect(verify(req(body, { "x-acme-timestamp": ts, "x-acme-signature": sig })).event_type)
      .toBe("message.received");
  });

  it("rejects a tampered body / stale timestamp / missing headers", () => { /* …toThrow(...) */ });
});
```

For an end-to-end check, drive the real path with `Spectrum(...)` +
`spectrum.webhook(...)`, encoding a `RawInboundEvent` whose `rawRequest` is your
platform's HTTP/1.1 wire bytes:

```typescript
const wire = `POST /acme HTTP/1.1\r\ncontent-type: application/json\r\n\r\n${body}`;
const event = RawInboundEvent.encode(
  RawInboundEvent.create({ eventId: "e1", projectId: "p", platform: "acme",
    rawRequest: new TextEncoder().encode(wire) })
).finish();

const spectrum = await Spectrum({ projectId: "p", projectSecret: "s", providers: [acme.config({ apiKey: "k" })] });
const result = await spectrum.webhook({ headers: { "content-type": "application/x-protobuf" }, body: event },
  (space, message) => { /* assert on the delivered [space, message] */ });
expect(result.status).toBe(200);
await spectrum.stop();
```

Cover the status matrix (200 success, 200 + body for a handshake, 400 on a
`verify` throw, 400 for an unknown platform).

---

## Tooling & PR checklist

PRs go to the SDK (`spectrum-ts`) or your adapter package — **not this repo**.

- **Lint/format:** both repos use [Ultracite](https://ultracite.ai) (Biome) —
  `pnpm run check` / `pnpm run fix`. A standalone adapter adds `tsc --noEmit` and
  `pnpm test`.
- **Commits:** [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) —
  `feat: add acme adapter`.
- **First-party (in `spectrum-ts`) only:** export it from
  `src/providers/index.ts`; it's then importable as `spectrum-ts/providers/<name>`
  via the `./providers/*` subpath — keep the directory name and subpath in sync.

Before opening a PR, confirm:

- [ ] The platform name is one constant used in all three places ([§routing](#platform-routing--the-name-invariant)).
- [ ] `verify` checks the signature against `rawBody` with a replay guard, and throws to reject.
- [ ] `messages` returns `undefined` for events you don't surface (incl. your own echoes).
- [ ] `send` handles every `content.type` you support and throws `UnsupportedError.content(...)` otherwise.
- [ ] Protocol handshakes (if any) are answered via `respond(...)`.
- [ ] `send`/`messages` are idempotent (delivery is at-least-once).
- [ ] `pnpm test` passes; `pnpm run check` is clean.
- [ ] README documents required config and the webhook URL/signature setup.

---

